  "query_preview": "the Coastal Flood Advisory in effect for Ocean County NJ \nsouth through Sussex and Kent Counties in Delaware through Wednesday \nevening. We also added New Castle DE and Salem County NJ in the \nAdvisory for Wednesday evening. In some of the back bays, moderate \ncoastal flooding is possible with Wednesday evening's high tide \ncycle. But given fluctuating forecasts, did not quite have the \nconfidence to issue a Coastal Flood Watch. Beyond Wednesday evening, \nthe nor'easter will gradually be moving away from the region for the \nend of the week. Another round of coastal flooding is possible \nThursday evening with spotty minor to widespread minor coastal \nflooding. This high tide cycle is forecast to be lower than \nWednesday evening's high tide cycle.\n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for the \nMaryland eastern shore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Frost impacts possible Friday night and Saturday \nnight where the growing season continues. \n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast on Friday, a \npotentially strong mid level ridge in the Plains to Midwest shifts \neastward some into the weekend. This will support strong surface \nhigh pressure initially centered near the Great Lakes, which then \nshifts eastward into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic Friday and over \nthe weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry air mass will accompany this strong surface high, \nand as the high itself settles more overhead the winds will decouple.\nThis will set the stage for efficient radiational cooling both Friday\nnight and Saturday night. The forecast lows, as of now, are in the\n30s for much of the area (away from the coasts and outside of the\nurban centers) with a frost potential. It is possible that if the\nradiational cooling ends up being stronger, then some areas \ncould get to freezing or a little below. Overall though, frost \nimpacts are possible where the growing season remains active.",
